2. The core once-off conversion
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Final pricing: Publisher R199 once-off, Business R399 once-off
(includes the R199 launch credit), commission 8% (was 12%, publisher
share 92%, was 88%).

Traced the real consequences of "no renewal, ever" through the actual
codebase rather than assuming a clean removal:
  - calculate_trust_score-style caution paid off again here: found that
    publisher_subscriptions/business_subscriptions' entire
    past_due/grace_period/suspended lifecycle, the payfast_token column,
    current_period_end, and a dedicated scheduled Edge Function
    (expire-subscription-grace-periods) all become meaningless the moment
    there's no recurring charge left to lapse from. Removed all of it
    rather than leaving vestigial columns/functions that could never be
    written to again — the same "still technically reachable" concern
    this whole exercise has been about elsewhere.
  - Four RLS policies plus one trigger (schema_phase71_subscription_
    enforcement.sql) hardcoded status in ('active', 'grace_period') as
    the "usable" check. Updated all five to status = 'active', matching
    the simplified isSubscriptionUsable() in subscriptions.ts.
  - business_launch_credits' own grant mechanism ("first-ever completed
    payment") needed no logic change at all — under a once-off model,
    "first-ever" and "the only" payment are the same event, so the exact
    same atomic check-and-set in payfast-notify still does the right
    thing.

